result:
I fixed search so only live content can be found, and the rebuild job now produces exactly the entries the rule allows. The full test suite passes (617 tests), and typecheck and lint are clean on the files I changed.

I also wrote a temporary scenario test for the support reports and deleted it after it passed. It covered:
- archiving an issue;
- archiving a project with and without its issues;
- comments and edits made after the project was archived;
- restoring the project;
- a rebuild in a workspace with 250 extra issues and comments, checking that another workspace's entries were untouched;
- stale entries inserted directly into the index.

**What changed:**
- **Search filters by liveness when it runs.** `src/server/repositories/search-repository.ts` checks every result against the projects, issues and comments tables. An entry only matches if it and everything above it are live, so the results and the total both obey the rule even if the index is out of date.
- **Index writers check liveness.** In `src/server/services/search-service.ts`, the functions that index issues, comments and projects now remove the entry instead of adding it when the item or anything above it is archived.
- **Archiving and restoring cascade.** Archiving an issue now removes its comments from search too. Archiving a project removes the project, all its issues and all their comments, whether or not the issues were archived. Restoring a project reindexes it along with any issues that are still live and their comments. Issues that were archived stay hidden.
- **The rebuild starts from scratch.** `src/server/jobs/search-reindex-job.ts` deletes only that workspace's entries, then pages through all live projects, their live issues and those issues' live comments. It no longer stops after the first 100 rows, which is why older content was missing in large workspaces.

**One addition beyond the reports:** editing a comment or a project didn't update search at all. So `comment-service.ts` and `project-service.ts` now send the existing `search.reindex_requested` event after an edit. A project edit reindexes all of that project's issues and comments, not just its own entry, which could be slow for very large projects.
